WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.875
“My number is eight one seven…” Pause.

2
00:00:03.195 --> 00:00:05.770
“Five five five…” Pause again.

3
00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:12.015
If your agent treats every silence as the end, a phone number becomes three conversations.

4
00:00:12.335 --> 00:00:16.260
Turn-taking begins with a probability, not a sentence.

5
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:22.055
Voice activity detection estimates whether each short audio frame contains speech.

6
00:00:22.375 --> 00:00:26.550
A minimum speech duration prevents a cough from opening a turn.

7
00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:30.695
A silence timer waits for enough quiet before closing it.

8
00:00:31.015 --> 00:00:35.790
These are useful signals, but silence inside a thought is still silence.

9
00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:40.060
On the waveform, mark the pauses inside the number in gold.

10
00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.480
A simple timer sees a possible endpoint.

11
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.600
The caller resumes.

12
00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:52.295
If the timer already committed, the system has cut the caller off and must repair the turn downstream.

13
00:00:52.615 --> 00:00:58.765
Semantic end-of-turn detection asks a different question: does this sound like a complete thought?

14
00:00:59.085 --> 00:01:02.635
It can combine the speech signal with transcript context.

15
00:01:02.955 --> 00:01:08.330
“My number is eight one seven” is grammatically unfinished, even after a pause.

16
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.175
“That’s everything I need” is more likely complete.

17
00:01:12.495 --> 00:01:14.870
This does not replace VAD.

18
00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:17.915
VAD says whether audio is active.

19
00:01:18.235 --> 00:01:22.285
Semantic detection estimates whether the idea is finished.

20
00:01:22.605 --> 00:01:27.205
Together they trade a little decision time for fewer premature cutoffs.

21
00:01:27.525 --> 00:01:35.125
For a booking call, test this on addresses, phone numbers, model numbers, and restarts—not just clean sentences.

22
00:01:35.445 --> 00:01:38.745
An interruption is not the same as a backchannel.

23
00:01:39.065 --> 00:01:41.115
“Uh-huh” may mean keep going.

24
00:01:41.435 --> 00:01:44.510
“No, the second floor” means stop and revise.

25
00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:50.130
A useful agent needs an interruption policy, not only a volume threshold.

26
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:54.925
When the caller interrupts, cancel response generation and stop playout.

27
00:01:55.245 --> 00:02:01.445
But cancellation has two histories: what the model generated, and what the caller actually heard.

28
00:02:01.765 --> 00:02:06.015
Truncate the conversation at the audio boundary the caller reached.

29
00:02:06.335 --> 00:02:12.510
Otherwise the model may remember words that never played and answer as if the caller heard them.

30
00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:16.180
Every turn detector makes two kinds of mistake.

31
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.650
It can cut off a caller too early, or make a caller wait too long.

32
00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.645
Record both.

33
00:02:22.965 --> 00:02:30.715
Measure endpointing delay, premature cutoffs, interruption response time, and the gap after a caller really finishes.

34
00:02:31.035 --> 00:02:33.535
Slice the results by utterance type.

35
00:02:33.855 --> 00:02:38.980
A detector that looks good on ordinary sentences can still fail on digits.

36
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:55.350
Two questions: **How should uncertainty about turn completion change the agent’s behavior?** And **which false interruption costs the caller the most?** Explore them in lab 03, then see how lab 01 section 4 cancels and truncates an interrupted response.
